    "turn_idx": 57,
    "turn_text": "Well, the intent to commit a crime belongs in the eyes and actions of the offender, not the person being offended. The crime is committed if a certain person decides to do something and the intent is in the offender, not in the offended. So if the offended says, Oh, that hurt my feelings. That's a crime. I'm sorry. That is not a crime. The crime comes from the intent of the offender. So a person who has their feelings hurt by something somebody says, cannot claim, oh, that person committed a crime against me because I was offended. No, not at the attempt and the other person's mind was not to commit a crime. So we hold the person accountable who did the action, not the person who receives some offense. You know, it's not a crime to be offended into United States.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_05",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
